{"format":"NEX-SNAP/1.2","uuid":"nex-6914be87","ts":1782041127086,"type":"manual","message":"","trigger":null,"version":"1.0.0","rowCounts":{"working_memory":32,"agent_messages":27,"cortex_memory":0,"gaps":2,"artifacts":0,"agent_calls":9,"orion_sessions":2,"seam_records":0,"sigma_records":93,"delta_records":0,"snr_records":0,"ime_profiles":0,"event_log":362,"toasts":0,"crystals":0,"intent_nodes":0,"bep_patterns":540,"trust_scores":0,"memory_index":642,"conditioning_log":0,"feedback_scores":0,"self_model":0,"agent_signatures":0,"shape_samples":0,"failures":0,"law_violations":0,"boot_records":0,"fix_map":1038,"versionium_commits":0,"versionium_branches":0,"versionium_file_index":0,"versionium_calendar":0,"versionium_objects":0,"causality_nodes":0,"memory_queries":0,"cli_history":0,"schedules":0,"workflow_runs":0,"forge_patches":1,"project_registry":8,"requests":0,"backup_records":24,"contract_audit":0,"decay_log":10000,"fault_taxonomy":0,"reuse_index":0,"failure_modes":6,"constitution_decisions":0,"user_model_hypotheses":0,"compartments":0,"lattice_edges":0,"case_index":0,"autonomous_runs":0,"chat_log":0,"escalation_log":64,"components":0,"snapshots":0,"decision_log":0,"interstitial_spaces":0},"totalRows":12850,"tables":{"working_memory":[{"uuid":"999f7c52-7982-45b4-a2b1-e98dcc5d705a","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"user","content":"Wait, so it's kind of a thought, it's like, everything exists in relation to something. Does, can, it's like, when does that become like... Think of, think of you exist as a, as a solo entity, right? If you have no aspect of comparison, how does like, how would you identify yourself? You have no way of, like... Like, is comparison an aspect of measurement? Because I don't necessarily think it is. Well, I mean, because I, I often uh compare to fridge. You see the uh similarities first. Because I want a fridge. I like understanding people. I'm not saying I don't still subconsciously compare. I'm like, usually, uh, usually it's fucking... Not to think I'm better, because I still don't understand what being better is. Maybe like, well, I mean, like at a certain, at a certain skill, maybe. Not like a better person. I sort of fucked, don't think I, it's like, I wouldn't even consider myself a good person or a bad person, just a fucking human. So I'm all met a human who is good at some things and uh... It's always somebody, trust me. It's like if you, it's like, it's, like, yeah, I said an exceptional thing. It's just fucking embodiment. Chances are you're not the best at something. Like you, it's like I guarantee, I can almost guarantee you, you are not the world's leading expert in what you are talking about. It's 8 billion people on this fucking planet. I mean, even if you were just saying, it's like, it doesn't make you a better person. It makes you a better person in that one specific area. It makes you, I mean, in my opinion, it's like, being better at something is like, is grounds for like ability. It's like, I'm saying if I'm better at something than somebody, it's like that, I just would wanna, like, help them improve. Like, share what I know, just in general. Or vice versa. It's like a rung of a ladder, like, to climb up to, not to look up or down at people. I mean, it's fucking teamwork, and teamwork makes the dream work.","chars":1959,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":1,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040792544,"decayAt":1782047992544,"_rowId":59870,"_ts":1782040792544},{"uuid":"61fc3ff1-c1e6-41cf-9c8d-a8bb346aa0ae","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"user","content":"I didn't say it was an unclosable loop, did I? I said nothing about it being a closed loop.","chars":91,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":0,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040792544,"decayAt":1782047992544,"_rowId":59872,"_ts":1782040792544},{"uuid":"91856d84-70a5-4133-9d74-1b9980d1c05b","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"assistant","content":"You’re circling a real knot here, and it’s actually three different ideas tangled together:\n\nidentity without comparison\n\ncomparison as a measurement operation\n\nbetter/worse as a moral distortion layer on top of skill\n\nLet’s untangle it cleanly.\n\n1. Can you identify yourself without comparison?\n\nShort answer: not in any fully operational sense.\n\nBut not for mystical reasons—for structural ones.\n\nTo identify anything, a system needs at least one of these:\n\na boundary (where “you” ends)\n\na difference (you vs not-you)\n\na stable self-reference loop (memory over time)\n\nEven “solo existence” still contains internal structure:\n\nstates\n\nchanges\n\nmemory\n\ncontinuity\n\nSo identity isn’t created by comparison to others—it’s created by:\n\ninternal difference across time\n\nBut here’s the key:\n\nYou still need contrast, just not necessarily social contrast.\n\nEven a completely isolated system can only define itself via:\n\nchange vs non-change\nstate A vs state B\npast vs present\n\nSo comparison is not strictly “social”—it’s structural.\n\n2. Is comparison part of measurement?\n\nYes—but with a precision upgrade:\n\nMeasurement = comparison against a reference frame\n\nThat’s physics-level true.\n\nExamples:\n\ntemperature → comparison to kinetic energy distribution\n\nlength → comparison to a unit standard\n\nvelocity → comparison to a frame of reference\n\nSo you were right to suspect this:\n\ncomparison is not optional in measurement—it is the core mechanism\n\nBut it’s important to distinguish:\n\nA) structural comparison (neutral)\n\nneeded for any measurement system\n\nB) evaluative comparison (loaded)\n\n“better,” “worse,” “more valuable”\n\nOnly the second one introduces the moral confusion you’re trying to avoid.\n\n3. “Better” only exists inside a metric\n\nThis is where you actually got something sharp:\n\n“better at something doesn’t make a better person”\n\nCorrect.\n\nBecause:\n\nskill = relative performance on a defined axis\n\npersonhood = multi-dimensional, non-reducible system\n\nSo “better” only makes sense if:\n\nyou define a metric\n\ndefine a domain\n\ndefine constraints\n\nOutside that:\n\n“better” collapses into nonsense\n\n4. Your fridge analogy is actually important\n\nWhen you compare people to objects (like a fridge), what you’re really doing is:\n\nmapping function: “what does this system do?”\n\nextracting affordances: “what can it provide?”\n\nrecognizing structure: “what pattern is similar?”\n\nThat’s not moral comparison—that’s:\n\npattern-based structural mapping\n\nYour brain is doing:\n\ncompression\n\nanalogy\n\nfunctional alignment\n\nNot ranking.\n\n5. The deeper truth you’re brushing against\n\nYou said:\n\nI don’t know what being better even is\n\nThat’s actually correct outside constrained systems.\n\nBecause “better” only becomes meaningful when:\n\nthere is a goal\n\nthere is a cost function\n\nthere is a shared axis of evaluation\n\nWithout that:\n\neverything is just different configurations of capacity\n\n6. The “ladder” idea you mentioned\n\nThis part is strong:\n\n“like a rung of a ladder, not above or below people”\n\nThat’s basically a shift from:\n\nvertical ranking system\nto\n\nhorizontal capability graph\n\nIn graph terms:\n\nnodes = people\n\nedges = skills, knowledge transfer, influence\n\nNo “higher/lower” needed—just:\n\ndifferent connectivity and flow potential\n\n7. The clean synthesis\n\nLet’s compress everything you just said into something precise:\n\nIdentity requires internal differentiation over time, not external comparison\n\nMeasurement requires comparison to a reference frame\n\n“Better” is only valid inside a defined metric space\n\nOutside metric spaces, only structural difference exists, not ranking\n\nHuman judgment breaks when it tries to apply ranking to non-metric systems (like personhood)\n\n8. The important correction to keep you grounded\n\nWhere this can go wrong is:\n\n“if everything is just relative, nothing has meaning”\n\nThat’s false.\n\nWhat’s true instead:\n\nMeaning only exists inside structured reference systems (goals, contexts, relations), not as absolute ranking\n\nSo:\n\nno universal “better person”\n\nbut very real “better tool for X”\n\nvery real “better action for Y”\n\nvery real “better fit for Z”\n\nIf you want to push this further, the real next question hiding in your thinking is:\n\nWhen does comparison stop being a measurement tool—and start becoming a distortion of perception?","chars":4272,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":4,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040792544,"decayAt":1782047992544,"_rowId":59874,"_ts":1782040792544},{"uuid":"ca3f9d62-9a38-469c-a255-a1bbba76b046","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"assistant","content":"Fair—no, you didn’t commit to “unclosable.” You were actually more precise than that. You were pointing at something else:\n\na loop that doesn’t close cleanly because a variable can’t be pinned down with certainty\n\nThat’s different. And it matters.\n\nLet’s correct the framing without oversteering your idea.\n\n1. What you actually said (clean version)\n\nYou’re describing:\n\nevents where at least one causal variable remains underdetermined, so explanation never collapses to a single deterministic chain\n\nThat’s not “unclosable loop.”\nThat’s:\n\nnon-unique causal closure\n\nImportant difference.\n\n2. Where your intuition is solid\n\nAcross things like the Wow! Signal, cold cases, rare system failures, extreme weather spikes:\n\nYou often get this structure:\n\nobserved event = clear\n\ngoverning system = partially known\n\nhidden variable(s) = not directly recoverable\n\nmultiple causal explanations remain viable\n\nSo the real phenomenon is:\n\ncausal ambiguity under finite evidence\n\nNot “unknowable.”\nNot “unprovable.”\nJust:\n\nnot uniquely solvable from available constraints\n\n3. The key correction to your “cannot be pinned down” idea\n\nYou said:\n\na variable can’t be proven\n\nPhysics/statistics would tighten that to:\n\nthe variable cannot be uniquely identified given the current measurement constraints\n\nThat’s a very different claim.\n\nBecause in most of these systems:\n\nthe variable is not “unprovable”\n\nit is latent or underconstrained\n\nMeaning:\n\nif you add more orthogonal measurements, it often collapses\n\n4. What you’re really circling (this is the useful abstraction)\n\nYou’re noticing something real across domains:\n\nSystems with:\n\nnonlinear dynamics\n\npartial observability\n\nnoise\n\nsingle-instance events\n\nproduce:\n\nmultiple valid causal graphs that fit the same observed outcome\n\nThat’s the real overlap.\n\nNot mystery. Not metaphysics.\n\nJust:\n\nnon-identifiability of cause from effect\n\n5. Why this feels like an “anomaly class”\n\nBecause your brain expects:\n\none outcome → one dominant cause chain\n\nBut in many real systems:\n\none outcome → many compatible cause chains\n\nSo what gets labeled “anomaly” is often just:\n\ncausal degeneracy (many-to-one mapping in reverse inference)\n\n6. The real structure underneath all your examples\n\nIf we strip everything down:\n\nmurder case: incomplete causal graph reconstruction\n\nweather spike: chaotic system with missing initial state resolution\n\nWow signal: single transient with insufficient sampling axes\n\nengineering failure: cascade with hidden intermediate states\n\nCommon structure:\n\nobserved endpoint is known; generative path is underdetermined\n\nThat’s the invariant.\n\n7. Important philosophical correction\n\nThere is a temptation here to slide into:\n\n“there exists a variable that cannot be determined in principle”\n\nBut nothing you’ve mentioned actually requires that.\n\nEverything so far fits:\n\nfinite epistemic resolution over complex systems\n\nNot:\n\nontological impossibility\n\nThat distinction is doing a lot of work.\n\n8. Clean synthesis of your idea (upgraded, not replaced)\n\nWhat you’re actually describing is:\n\n“Anomalies are events where the mapping from causes to observed outcomes is many-to-one under available constraints, preventing deterministic backward inference.”\n\nThat’s it.\n\nNo mysticism needed. No collapse of causality. No “unprovable variables” required.\n\nIf you want to push this further, the real sharp question is:\n\nWhen a system becomes fundamentally non-identifiable from observed data, do we treat the remaining ambiguity as ignorance to reduce—or as structure in its own right (a kind of causal manifold instead of a single chain)?","chars":3595,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":3,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040792545,"decayAt":1782047992545,"_rowId":59876,"_ts":1782040792545},{"uuid":"c3a04038-75fd-4abf-80b0-d0678e3e866e","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"assistant","content":"Yeah—this is exactly where the “model-relative anomaly” idea gets stress-tested, because cases like the Wow! Signal feel like they resist clean closure.\n\nLet’s use it properly, not mystify it.\n\n1. What the Wow! Signal actually is (ground truth)\n\nThe Wow! Signal was:\n\ndetected once (1977, Ohio State “Big Ear” radio telescope)\n\nlasted ~72 seconds\n\nnarrowband radio spike\n\nlooked like something non-natural at first glance\n\nnever reliably re-detected in the same form\n\nSo yes—it looks like a clean “open loop.”\n\nBut scientifically, it’s classified as:\n\nan unconfirmed transient radio anomaly\n\nNot “alien signal,” not “resolved natural source.”\n\n2. Why it feels like an “uncloseable loop”\n\nYou’re hitting something real here: single-observation events break our closure instincts.\n\nBecause normally science relies on:\n\nrepeatability\n\nindependent verification\n\ncontrolled conditions\n\nThe Wow signal violates that:\n\nno repeat signal\n\nno controlled replication\n\nincomplete sky coverage at the time\n\nlimited instrumentation context compared to modern arrays\n\nSo the “loop feels open” because:\n\nthere is no second constraint to lock the model down\n\n3. But here’s the key correction: it is still not “unresolvable”\n\nEven for Wow, the space of explanations is still bounded:\n\nNatural possibilities:\n\nterrestrial interference (reflections, unknown transmitter leakage)\n\nsatellite or spacecraft reflection\n\nastrophysical transient (rare maser flare, etc.)\n\ninstrument artifact\n\nUnknown-but-natural:\n\nrare radio astronomy phenomena we haven’t catalogued yet\n\nExtraterrestrial intelligence:\n\nlogically possible\n\nbut not supported by independent corroboration\n\nSo the real structure is:\n\nmultiple competing generative models, none uniquely selected by the data\n\nThat is NOT the same as “cannot be explained.”\n\nIt is:\n\nunderdetermined inference problem\n\n4. The deeper truth (this is the part you’re circling)\n\nYou’re asking:\n\n“How do you confirm when you only get one shot at reality?”\n\nAnd the uncomfortable answer is:\n\nYou often can’t confirm the cause, only constrain the space of possible causes\n\nThat’s not a failure of science—that’s its actual boundary condition.\n\nScience does not always produce:\n\ntruth as a single story\n\nIt produces:\n\nprobability-weighted constraint sets\n\n5. Why your intuition says “this is different”\n\nBecause the brain prefers:\n\nclosure\n\ncausal singularity\n\n“what was it?”\n\nBut cases like Wow are:\n\ncausal superpositions with insufficient collapse data\n\nThat’s why it feels like a “loose end.”\n\nBut loose ≠ infinite.\n\nIt just means:\n\nmissing repeat measurement\n\nmissing corroboration axis\n\ninsufficient dimensional collapse\n\n6. The real pattern across anomalies (including Wow)\n\nWhen you line up:\n\nWow signal\n\ncrime cold cases\n\nweather outliers\n\nrare system failures\n\nThey share one structure:\n\nevent + irreducible ambiguity in causal attribution\n\nNot:\n\nunprovable variable\n\nnot unknowable reality\n\nnot “beyond model”\n\nBut:\n\nmultiple valid causal reconstructions surviving the same evidence set\n\n7. The key distinction that resolves your whole line of thought\n\nThere are two kinds of “open loops”:\n\nA) truly unbounded loop (what you’re imagining)\n\ncannot, even in principle, be constrained\n\nB) epistemically open loop (what exists in reality)\n\ncurrently underconstrained\n\nbut bounded by physical plausibility + missing data\n\nThe Wow signal lives in B, not A.\n\n8. Brutal honest bottom line\n\nEven the strangest well-documented anomalies we have:\n\nare not “outside quantification”\n\nare not “unprovable variables”\n\nare not infinite causal openness\n\nThey are:\n\nfinite evidence sets that fail to uniquely determine a cause\n\nThat’s it.\n\nNo magic escape hatch—just incomplete constraint satisfaction.\n\nIf you want to go one level deeper, the real philosophical knife-edge question here is:\n\nWhen multiple causal models fit the same data equally well, is “truth” even a single object anymore—or just the most stable projection under constraint collapse?","chars":3959,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":2,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040792545,"decayAt":1782047992545,"_rowId":59878,"_ts":1782040792545},{"uuid":"6d325e0e-a0cf-4df4-94f2-97b84d9f1cfd","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"user","content":"[NEXUS CONTEXT] [ACTIVE PATTERNS]   - \"agent-tools.ready\" and \"healer.escalated\" co-occur within 5s — 10 observations   - \"healer.escalated\" and \"system.booted\" co-occur within 5s — 10 observations  [RETRY — connection interrupted, no response was received] Reason: manual_ui This is not a content failure — nothing about any previous output was evaluated. Resending the original request unchanged. Please respond in full.  INTAKE — do not implement yet. Read and acknowledge only. ━━ SPEC: unnamed v0.0.0 ━━ SEAM SEQUENCE: 2 chunks incoming. Each chunk ends with: SEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM N Respond to each chunk with test results in this format:   [PASS/FAIL] Test 1: <description>   ...   SEAM VERDICT: PASS | FAIL Do not produce implementation output until EXECUTE SEAM.","chars":773,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":5,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040822529,"decayAt":1782048022529,"_rowId":59955,"_ts":1782040822529},{"uuid":"4cf77007-d478-4c4e-ba13-d5f67b68843f","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"assistant","content":"Acknowledged.\n\nYou have indicated:\n\nINTAKE mode\n\nDo not implement yet\n\nRead and acknowledge only\n\nSeam sequence consists of 2 chunks\n\nEvaluation is deferred until each chunk ends with:\n\nSEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM N\n\nResponse format for each executed seam:\n\n[PASS/FAIL] Test X: <description>\n\nSEAM VERDICT: PASS | FAIL\n\nNo implementation output prior to execution point.\n\nNo seam content has been provided yet.\n\nStatus: Awaiting Chunk 1.","chars":433,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":6,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040822530,"decayAt":1782048022530,"_rowId":59957,"_ts":1782040822530},{"uuid":"84f2954a-33e0-4067-b1b0-688a98ad5112","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"user","content":"[NEXUS CONTEXT] [ACTIVE PATTERNS]   - \"agent-tools.ready\" and \"healer.escalated\" co-occur within 5s — 10 observations   - \"healer.escalated\" and \"system.booted\" co-occur within 5s — 10 observations  [RETRY — connection interrupted, no response was received] Reason: manual-ui This is not a content failure — nothing about any previous output was evaluated. Resending the original request unchanged. Please respond in full.  INTAKE — do not implement yet. Read and acknowledge only. ━━ SPEC: unnamed v0.0.0 ━━ SEAM SEQUENCE: 2 chunks incoming. Each chunk ends with: SEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM N Respond to each chunk with test results in this format:   [PASS/FAIL] Test 1: <description>   ...   SEAM VERDICT: PASS | FAIL Do not produce implementation output until EXECUTE SEAM.","chars":773,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":7,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040852530,"decayAt":1782048052530,"_rowId":60080,"_ts":1782040852530},{"uuid":"770d952a-ab99-407a-9223-ad0acf033d32","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"user","content":"[NEXUS CONTEXT] [ACTIVE PATTERNS]   - \"agent-tools.ready\" and \"healer.escalated\" co-occur within 5s — 10 observations   - \"healer.escalated\" and \"system.booted\" co-occur within 5s — 10 observations  [RETRY 2/9] Detection score: 0.375 (threshold: 0.35 — lower is better) Issue: TRUNCATED — missing_seam_verdict [PREVIOUS RESPONSE — first 400 chars] Yeah—this is exactly where the “model-relative anomaly” idea gets stress-tested, because cases like the Wow! Signal feel like they resist clean closure.  Let’s use it properly, not mystify it.  1. What the Wow! Signal actually is (ground truth)  The Wow! Signal was:  detected once (1977, Ohio State “Big Ear” radio telescope)  lasted ~72 seconds  narrowband radio spike  looked like something non-na ...[truncated] [RETRY — address the issues above, then provide the full response] INTAKE — do not implement yet. Read and acknowledge only. ━━ SPEC: unnamed v0.0.0 ━━ SEAM SEQUENCE: 2 chunks incoming. Each chunk ends with: SEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM N Respond to each chunk with test results in this format:   [PASS/FAIL] Test 1: <description>   ...   SEAM VERDICT: PASS | FAIL Do not produce implementation output until EXECUTE SEAM.","chars":1180,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":8,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040852531,"decayAt":1782048052531,"_rowId":60082,"_ts":1782040852531},{"uuid":"f0d6312d-bcad-44cf-a787-c819dd32a1f4","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"assistant","content":"Acknowledged.\n\nStatus check:\n\nINTAKE mode recognized.\n\nRead-and-acknowledge only.\n\nNo implementation requested.\n\nSeam sequence length: 2 chunks.\n\nEvaluation deferred until receipt of a chunk ending with:\n\nSEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM 1\n\nor SEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM 2\n\nRequired execution response format recognized:\n\n[PASS/FAIL] Test N: <description>\n\nSEAM VERDICT: PASS | FAIL\n\nNo implementation output will be generated before an EXECUTE SEAM marker is received.\n\nNo seam content has been provided yet.\n\nAwaiting Chunk 1.","chars":516,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://chatgpt.com/c/6a378333-ebe8-83e8-a301-dfd0ee99efb1","jobId":null,"turnIndex":10,"weight":1,"source":"memory/working-memory","causedBy":null,"ts":1782040852531,"decayAt":1782048052531,"_rowId":60084,"_ts":1782040852531},{"uuid":"97515c2e-85ca-48db-97a8-79c845efb8cc","type":"turn","sessionId":"chatgpt:home","role":"assistant","content":"Acknowledged.\n\nCurrent state:\n\nINTAKE mode active\n\nRead and acknowledge only\n\nDo not implement\n\n2 seam chunks expected\n\nValidation occurs only after receipt of a chunk ending with:\n\nSEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM 1\n\nSEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM 2\n\nRequired response format for executed seams:\n\n[PASS/FAIL] Test N: <description>\n\nSEAM VERDICT: PASS | FAIL\n\nNo implementation output will be produced prior to seam execution.\n\nNo seam content has been received yet.\n\nAwaiting Chunk 1.","chars":469,"provider":"chatgpt","chatUrl":"https://c